WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.540
BRENT OGLE: I grew up only 30
minutes from Hillsdale College,

00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:05.910
and we would go and watch
sporting events there.

00:00:05.910 --> 00:00:07.260
[CHEERING]

00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:08.670
The mid to late
'80s in Hillsdale

00:00:08.670 --> 00:00:10.500
was a very good time
for the football team.

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:12.590
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:12.590 --> 00:00:15.710
I knew I wanted to play football
at the collegiate level.

00:00:15.710 --> 00:00:19.550
Hillsdale was gracious enough to
give me a scholarship to play,

00:00:19.550 --> 00:00:21.890
and I played all
four years there.

00:00:21.890 --> 00:00:24.380
Really, football,
for me, allowed

00:00:24.380 --> 00:00:25.640
me to come out of my shell.

00:00:25.640 --> 00:00:31.760
I was shy, quiet,
not very confident.

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:35.330
Football gave me
confidence, and it totally

00:00:35.330 --> 00:00:38.210
changed the game for me.

00:00:38.210 --> 00:00:40.010
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:40.010 --> 00:00:44.010
From a long way back, my
dad was a very hard worker.

00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:48.050
He passed that along to me, and
then, the football background,

00:00:48.050 --> 00:00:49.650
I think it all kind
of plays together

00:00:49.650 --> 00:00:52.220
that I wasn't going to give up.

00:00:52.220 --> 00:00:55.730
So then, fast forward
to my time at Welch.

00:00:55.730 --> 00:00:57.080
They make packaging.

00:00:57.080 --> 00:00:58.550
We're not talking a few.

00:00:58.550 --> 00:01:01.460
They're doing millions a day.

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:04.730
An opportunity came up to
be a general manager, which

00:01:04.730 --> 00:01:08.495
was the ultimate goal, run
a branch, run a facility.

00:01:08.495 --> 00:01:15.740
At 27 years old, I come in to
a operation of about 45 people.

00:01:15.740 --> 00:01:18.230
One guy, literally, his
nickname for me in the plant

00:01:18.230 --> 00:01:21.980
was Beaver Cleaver just because
I was so young, just a baby.

00:01:21.980 --> 00:01:24.120
That business was losing money.

00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:25.220
They needed a spark.

00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:27.330
And so I was going
to provide the spark.

00:01:30.170 --> 00:01:35.090
First thing I did is I worked
alongside everyone there.

00:01:35.090 --> 00:01:36.110
I learned their jobs.

00:01:36.110 --> 00:01:37.040
I did the jobs.

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:41.180
I remember moving
out there, and I was

00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:45.350
working endless hours a day.

00:01:45.350 --> 00:01:48.230
Chicago has its stresses
just being in Chicago.

00:01:48.230 --> 00:01:50.690
You can't get trucks
in and out of places.

00:01:50.690 --> 00:01:52.640
Drive an hour and a
half in a traffic jam

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:54.980
to get to work to deal
with the stresses of work,

00:01:54.980 --> 00:01:57.710
and then you go home, and you
drive another hour and a half.

00:01:57.710 --> 00:01:58.730
You get home at 8:00.

00:01:58.730 --> 00:02:01.530
You don't have time to have
dinner with your family.

00:02:01.530 --> 00:02:02.960
And so I was missing out.

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:06.500
I was in the car, or I was at
work more than I was at home.

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:09.490
That culminated in
a lot of stress.

00:02:09.490 --> 00:02:13.810
One time in particular where
the stress, it got to me,

00:02:13.810 --> 00:02:16.330
and I almost blacked out.

00:02:16.330 --> 00:02:20.650
I started to go out,
and I caught myself.

00:02:20.650 --> 00:02:25.630
And I started thinking, is
it worth blacking out over?

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:33.590
We're in Walmart.

00:02:33.590 --> 00:02:34.720
We come out.

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:38.770
We sit down, and I have an
emotional breakdown to my wife.

00:02:38.770 --> 00:02:41.380
LINDSAY OGLE: We were in the
car together by ourselves

00:02:41.380 --> 00:02:43.652
without the kids, and
I thought he was just

00:02:43.652 --> 00:02:45.110
going to say
something, and we were

00:02:45.110 --> 00:02:46.120
going to get out of the
car, and we were going

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:47.620
to continue on our errand.

00:02:47.620 --> 00:02:53.530
And then I realized very
quickly that he was struggling.

00:02:53.530 --> 00:02:55.840
BRENT OGLE: I'm bawling,
and I'm just so emotional.

00:02:55.840 --> 00:03:00.220
And I said I just feel trapped.

00:03:00.220 --> 00:03:02.410
LINDSAY OGLE: I knew
that work was stressful,

00:03:02.410 --> 00:03:05.860
but I didn't realize
where he was emotionally.

00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:08.770
BRENT OGLE: I just feel
I can't, I can't do this.

00:03:08.770 --> 00:03:14.560
I was just talking to her,
and I just had to unload that.

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:15.460
And she had no idea.

00:03:15.460 --> 00:03:16.600
I'd held it all inside.

00:03:16.600 --> 00:03:18.100
LINDSAY OGLE: I
just thought, oh, my

00:03:18.100 --> 00:03:19.900
gosh, how did we get
ourselves in this situation?

00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:20.942
None of this is worth it.

00:03:24.730 --> 00:03:27.760
BRENT OGLE: In my gut, it had
been stirring for about a year.

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:31.330
I wanted to have my
own business, something

00:03:31.330 --> 00:03:33.700
that I could call my own,
something that I could build,

00:03:33.700 --> 00:03:35.761
a legacy.

00:03:35.761 --> 00:03:38.450
So I left Welch Packaging.

00:03:38.450 --> 00:03:41.290
And our plan was one
year, in one year

00:03:41.290 --> 00:03:42.730
to find a business to buy.

00:03:45.390 --> 00:03:49.170
The business that I own is
a sporting goods company,

00:03:49.170 --> 00:03:50.970
Blazer Athletic Equipment.

00:03:50.970 --> 00:03:55.680
We make equipment for
high schools and colleges.

00:03:55.680 --> 00:03:57.510
Our primary sport
is track and field.

00:04:01.210 --> 00:04:03.400
My customers that
I deal with are

00:04:03.400 --> 00:04:07.360
dealing with high school
sports, high school athletes,

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:09.250
and collegiate teams.

00:04:09.250 --> 00:04:14.620
I get to talk sports with them
at trade shows and in meetings

00:04:14.620 --> 00:04:18.110
and couldn't imagine
anything better.

00:04:18.110 --> 00:04:21.380
I didn't realize when I
was there how important

00:04:21.380 --> 00:04:25.910
and how great Hillsdale was,
and football gave me confidence.

00:04:25.910 --> 00:04:29.960
In that aggressive arena,
hung up the cleats,

00:04:29.960 --> 00:04:31.850
and I can't play anymore.

00:04:31.850 --> 00:04:35.540
At 40, even if I tried, I'd
be sore for a couple weeks

00:04:35.540 --> 00:04:36.930
or maybe hospitalized.

00:04:36.930 --> 00:04:41.250
So it switch from a
football field to an office.

00:04:41.250 --> 00:04:42.750
Life is full of hard things.

00:04:42.750 --> 00:04:44.640
There's always adversity.

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:47.010
There's always problems
you're going to encounter.

00:04:47.010 --> 00:04:50.760
That drives me to be
better myself, drives

00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:53.580
me to make the company better.

00:04:53.580 --> 00:04:57.600
That competitive nature is
what has given me the tools

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:01.100
professionally.

00:05:01.100 --> 00:05:03.850
There is a set of rules,
but at the end of the day,

00:05:03.850 --> 00:05:05.440
you're still trying to win.

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:07.320
And I love to wake
up and try to win.

00:05:07.320 --> 00:05:10.070
[MUSIC PLAYING]

